#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e49e2595-62a1-595a-8df9-99541bccbdf5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Permutations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a766b29-b33e-5bf8-99cc-a9498ac00a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282631} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ebdd40-eff2-5962-8c47-618659ba7e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282632} {:answer/id #uuid "3532685c-1e3b-5f20-9f37-c866ae3ee5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "119" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282633} {:answer/id #uuid "ade55d68-ad69-5778-be4b-a2b1ebed97ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282634} {:answer/id #uuid "62984d86-6720-54f4-83b7-4532c8f1946c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3532685c-1e3b-5f20-9f37-c866ae3ee5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "119" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282633} :answer-field/id #uuid "75328b98-6c82-5e9c-bb1c-60fc6ead4211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73f708e-ccdf-5d98-9325-8bdc4f82ae5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102889" :question/problem "How many different codes can be formed by the characters ${y,z,2,4,6}$ besides \"$yz246$\"? Each character can be used only once." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The total number of the characters is $5$, so there are $5!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe code \"$yz246$\" is one of them. By subtracting $1$ from $120,$ we find the number of the different codes that can be formed beside \"$yz246$\".\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120-1=119\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be6a682-090d-5740-b09a-b04c01b45b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282638} {:answer/id #uuid "32b003f6-061b-5082-b184-04f5019398ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282639} {:answer/id #uuid "3b84547a-277e-5d9e-92e4-9b6e76ae57ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282640} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ba990d-54a8-5b7b-a159-29a3453dab0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282641} {:answer/id #uuid "574037b9-99ef-5402-bf32-cbf071bbb2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1be6a682-090d-5740-b09a-b04c01b45b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282638} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a438b4a-4bcc-550d-b74d-e7ba2c96cf0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d44ab2d1-b7c8-5c21-9bb5-01b432266f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-150014" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\frac{3}{3x+1}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,0)$ is $m=3.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "352aa2a8-5555-5333-856a-2a46b33d96aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(2{e}^{x}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282645} {:answer/id #uuid "031214be-a46b-5306-9d5f-e82f68bca114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282646} {:answer/id #uuid "3908dc91-dc16-5891-b6f0-f15fdb9d639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x{e}^{x}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282647} {:answer/id #uuid "e887fe31-888e-5b61-af7c-27507a624531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282648} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6dfc71-ac25-5a81-98df-8ff01479f612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "031214be-a46b-5306-9d5f-e82f68bca114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282646} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fec161b-eca2-5ae3-81da-ac4ebf2dedae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77390db0-e13a-5db8-9b13-017e21f5f5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-1916" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{x})+({x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+2)x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f61a7c3-82e6-5f90-8a95-4a79078fd542" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f88284-0fd0-57d6-b2a3-9e8d18e48896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b7c6f2453026a6cdc4dbd91806108513a4522f3aa42bc73e4dcc68e1ae0013f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282652} {:answer/id #uuid "8e251494-a568-568b-baf1-f74c6dc5609f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/4e70aa30c11b1576699fa384e830d9ad1608963abb0ee98e0cd961d6afb2762c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282653} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f4c91c-03e4-5256-9490-424d241e0d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/72c4b7567924115d3717a14a98d5a1806687b68a6dafe7d102927d98e0d2a4c3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282654} {:answer/id #uuid "2001395f-da79-59c1-b806-1e2f7e299f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/f70c7f91f1164b564fea4582928392748a150e63f66b8038b7aad007e2760034.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282655} {:answer/id #uuid "05ec3b62-221d-5583-b938-fa7d81fec805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/78e1b3933cf1048d32fba849cf1fac5e8ef049fc0714cdf0512d912c9875424f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2001395f-da79-59c1-b806-1e2f7e299f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/f70c7f91f1164b564fea4582928392748a150e63f66b8038b7aad007e2760034.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282655} :answer-field/id #uuid "f165a68f-99a0-52a7-b701-45724d33c861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9dea313d-b72c-5519-83b1-358690e83675" :question/math-academy-id "q-317288" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{5}^{x}+4?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/09798cb8c62aabe483490ad24f4c13918138c4292ffb19a8d6612ab4efe18497.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{5}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units up to give $y=-{5}^{x}+4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5101f0dc-70c0-5b62-a2a9-f4da74e5e623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5101f0dc-70c0-5b62-a2a9-f4da74e5e623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5e7501-56e0-5ddf-8a32-892664f3997c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef999424-e263-53f1-bf60-39690b6d18bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-325983" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5d195b-bbfd-5035-8e28-dfa550cb4438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282662} {:answer/id #uuid "eae4ba94-e9b8-5bbb-bb69-20ded2bbfb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282663} {:answer/id #uuid "d94a3d52-0f16-5e25-ab40-30ba4a3ca954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282664} {:answer/id #uuid "91be3907-c716-5987-a5b3-09abc540e46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0f8337-ad59-568e-85d3-ac9595a6c899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d195b-bbfd-5035-8e28-dfa550cb4438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282662} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90b041d-9bd3-5018-a809-23259c9b6d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b94e205-84ae-5402-93e4-aef9ed193fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-40030" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8}{2x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,+\\infty ).$ What is the value of $b?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{2}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{2} & =4 \\\\ -b & =8 \\\\ b & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4c7eef-f32c-5c45-ada3-775718a01d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282669} {:answer/id #uuid "9608091a-fd2d-58b8-a4b9-2d319281afae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282670} {:answer/id #uuid "88b29c1a-6178-5cda-a89d-0e466bda990d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282671} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbb37d1-7078-508e-b8eb-dccdf6e1c076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "148\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282672} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee6067e-b2d8-5f2b-8ffb-c1fa7443c0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee6067e-b2d8-5f2b-8ffb-c1fa7443c0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282673} :answer-field/id #uuid "2661080f-2c65-5431-b05e-ccb9e40eddbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddd59ad-58ff-5804-a895-8e6dabf22244" :question/math-academy-id "q-62069" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b9668f2d9988488c64e2bb7edb55ae94b1b84b947e249fbc96c506b1c5095d4d.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces a triangular prism-shaped box to hold chocolate candies. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 24+2\\times 45+54 \\\\  & =48+90+54 \\\\  & =192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
